// Package essconform runs a synthesized ESS conformance suite against an implementation.
//
// Generated by `ess verify conform synthesize --target go`. Do not edit: change the specification
// and regenerate. The suite it runs is in suite.go beside this file.
//
// # What you write
//
// One type satisfying Target, and one test that hands it over:
//
//	func TestConformance(t *testing.T) {
//	    essconform.Run(t, func() essconform.Target { return newTarget() })
//	}
//
// Nothing else. Every scenario, every assertion and every diagnostic below is derived from the
// specification, so adding a command or an invariant to the model adds checks here without anybody
// editing a test.
//
// # The runner owns every source of variation
//
// There is no clock read, no random number and no identifier minted anywhere except in Harness,
// which the runner constructs and hands to the target in each request. A target that needs a
// correlation id or a deadline is given one. This is what makes two runs over one specification
// produce the same report on any machine, at any load, on any day.
//
// Nothing sleeps. An `eventually` step asks the target again and hands it the deadline; the waiting
// happens inside the target, which is the only layer that knows what it is waiting for. The same
// goes for a length of time a scenario claims: the runner states the length and asks Clock what it
// measured, and whether the target got there by waiting or by advancing a clock of its own is not
// something this file knows or asks.
package essconform

